From e3b96e0367b9b4c22a9a8cc139cfa04cb008e2ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 01:37:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 13/13] l2r-local: step D (R3): big integer constants and match
 keys reach MLIR in hex (LLVM's decimal parse is cubic)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Under rcgmp a 100k-digit Nat literal took rrc about 17 minutes, all of it
in MIR to MLIR, and the time grew with the cube of the length (20k digits
8 s, 80k 527 s). Codegen printed each `rcgmp.constant` value and each
`rcgmp.match` key as a decimal integer attribute (`bigint_constant` and
`narrowest`), and `rcgmp::match_arms` printed the keys back in decimal to
build the arms attribute; `Attribute::parse` reads decimal text through
LLVM's StringRef::consumeInteger, a full APInt multiplication per digit,
cubic in the length (still so on LLVM main). Hexadecimal text goes
through shifts.

New `literal::mlir_integer_attr_text` writes the attribute text in hex,
`0x…` or `-0x…` (malachite's LowerHex, linear) at the same narrowest
signed width; codegen uses it for constants and match keys, and
`rcgmp::match_arms` takes the keys' text instead of parsed attributes.
MLIR's attributes are the same: identical MLIR for a 10k-digit literal,
and a unit test compares the decimal and hex parses (and the arms
attribute) at the widths' edges, the immediate range and a limb, either
sign. A 100k-digit Nat or negative Int literal now compiles to an object
at -O aggressive in about 0.5 s.

Tests: frontend/bigint_huge_literal.rr (3^104795, 50000 digits, as a Nat
constant, a negative Int constant and two Nat match keys, generated by
Inputs/bigint_huge_literal.py; the compile runs under `timeout 120` and
the program compares every literal with the power computed at run time),
the reussir-codegen unit test, `mlir_integer_attr_text` cases in
reussir-core's literal tests.
